  "body": "**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**\nIn tequila we can convert Hamiltonians to matrices\n```python\nimport tequila as tq\n\nH = tq.QubitHamiltonian(\"X(0)+2*Y(1)Z(3)\")\nH = H*tq.paulis.Z(0)\n\nM = H.to_matrix()\n```\n\nfor circuits this is currently not possible, but would be great to have. \n\n**Describe the solution you'd like**\nA possible solution consists of two steps: \n1. Compile the circuit to the level of Pauli-gates\n2. Use the closed-form to get the matrix of a gate\n3. multiply all matrices from the circui\n\nHere is a small function skeleton that would do the job\n```python\nimport tequila as tq\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef c2m(circuit):\n    \n    # compile to CNOT and Pauli-Gates    \n    cc = tq.compile_circuit(circuit, exponential_pauli=False)\n    n = max(cc.qubits)+1\n    M = np.eye(n)\n    for gate in cc.gates:\n        generator = gate.make_generator(include_controls=True)\n        angle = None\n        if gate.is_parametrized(): angle = gate.parameter\n        m = ....\n        M = M.dot(m)\n    \n    return M\n\n```\n\nIf the generator consists of a single Pauli string $P$ (use `generator.paulistrings` which is just a list) the closed-form of the gate is $U(\\theta) = \\cos(\\theta/2) - i \\sin(\\theta/2) P$ which can be used to compute the small matrix m. For the cnot the generator are multiple Paulistrings, but they commute, so they can be converted into individual gates with a single Paulistring as generator (coefficients need to be included in the corresponding angles).\n\nThe function can in principle be implemented using full parametrization. One can use type of syntax:\n```python\na = tq.Variable(\"a\")\nf = (a/2).apply(tq.numpy.cos)\n```\n\nAlternatively, one forces all parameters to be numbers\n```python\nvariables = {\"a\":1.0, ....}\ncircuit = circuit.map_variables(variables)\n```\n\n**Describe alternatives you've considered**\nusing `g = generator.to_matrix()` which is already implemented and then just brute force the exponential form $U(\\theta) = e^{-i \\theta/2 G}$ would work as well (less efficient though). \n\n**Additional context**\nProbably helpful to check out the [tutorial on tq circuits](https://tequilahub.github.io/tequila-tutorials/tutorials/tq-circuits/) with additional information on how the parametrization works.\n",
